WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.820
Tim: Good morning and welcome. My name is
Tim Caspar. I work at Hillsdale College, where

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.759
I serve as Associated Vice President for external
affairs, and deputy editor of 'Imprimis'.

00:00:10.759 --> 00:00:15.900
I also have the privilege of teaching in the
politics department. I'm also a graduate of

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:22.900
the college 1994 which is 21 years ago in
May, starting to seem like a long time ago.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.300
Mowing over themes like the passage of time
and advancing age reminded me of one of my

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.430
political heroes who Charles Krauthammer mentioned
last night, Ronald Reagan. Reagan was constantly

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:38.540
fending off concerns about his age with grace
and humor during the 1980 campaign. When the

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.540
issue came up again in 1984, he made a point
of saying famously during a debate with Walter

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:48.540
Mondale, "I will not make age an issue of
this campaign. I'm not going to exploit for

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:55.330
political purposes my opponents' youth and
inexperience." Again, welcome. I'll be your

00:00:55.330 --> 00:01:00.390
MC today. Welcome to this 'National Leadership
Seminar’ in Churchill's true greatness lessons

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.739
for today.
Before introducing our first speaker, I'd

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:09.530
like to point out that the college has a literature
table set up out in the hallway. You can find

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:14.670
information out there about many college programs
on and off campus. You can also find copies

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.900
of 'Imprimis', the college's national speech
digest. I'm happy to report that the monthly

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.800
circulation of 'Imprimis' has grown from a
few thousand in 1972 when it began to over

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.800
2.9 million today, and we have our sites set
on on three million. (Applause)

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:39.420
Our first speaker today is Barry Singer. Mr.
Singer is the proprietor of Chartwell Booksellers

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.450
in New York City, the only standing bookshop
in the world devoted to the writings of Winston

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:50.020
Churchill. As a book seller, he has handled
first editions of all 42 books that Winston

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:53.960
Churchill published in his lifetime. I made
a point to ask him at dinner last night if

00:01:53.960 --> 00:02:03.140
the number 42 is correct. He assured me Richard
Langworth would agree.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.490
He's also handled many of the most significant
Churchill rarities to come to light over the

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:12.329
past three decades, including a long-lost
handwritten manuscript for a work of fiction

00:02:12.329 --> 00:02:17.029
and short story entitled, 'On the Flank of
the Army'. Mr. Singer writes extensively on

00:02:17.029 --> 00:02:21.340
the arts for numerous newspapers and magazines,
and he is the author of several books on the

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.939
subject, including 'Alive at the Village Vanguard',
which won an ‘ASCAP Deems Taylor Award’

00:02:25.939 --> 00:02:32.939
for excellence in music writing. His latest
book, 'Churchill Style: The Art of Being Winston

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:36.700
Churchill' is his first on the subject of
Winston Churchill. The book's theme as he

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.870
writes in his introduction is "The artistry
he employed in constructing his day-to-day

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:49.819
life." This theme is the subject of his presentation
today. Please join me in welcoming Mr. Barry

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:58.709
Singer. (Applause)
Barry: Good morning.

00:02:58.709 --> 00:03:01.559
Audience: Good morning.
Barry: Thank you very much for coming out

00:03:01.559 --> 00:03:06.650
so early to listen to me talk to you about
Winston Churchill in the morning. I'm delighted

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:12.599
to be here. Winston Churchill would not have
been. Winston Churchill was not a morning

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:21.879
person. He worked at night. He worked until
very late into the night, usually dictating

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:32.099
his speeches, his books, his magazine articles
to a rotating retinue of very dizzy secretaries

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:39.099
who tried to keep up with him.
He went to bed late, and he didn't rise until

00:03:39.329 --> 00:03:47.809
probably around this time, if not little later.
When he did, he was brought the morning papers,

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:54.219
every one of them on this. This is one of
many bed trays that Winston Churchill employed

00:03:54.219 --> 00:04:00.799
throughout his life. I found it in the attic
at Chartwell. They would bring him the papers,

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:05.139
and they would bring him an enormous hot breakfast.
I hope you had a breakfast this morning, and

00:04:05.139 --> 00:04:10.529
remotely as large as the ones that Winston
Churchill consumed every morning.

00:04:10.529 --> 00:04:15.989
One of his pre-war secretaries, a lady by
the name of Phyllis Moir once described watching

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:22.989
Churchill in the morning, "In his old-fashioned
four-poster bed, the Peter Pan of British

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:32.889
politics", her words, "Sitting up, tufts of
red hair standing up from his head, scanning

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:39.889
closely the morning newspapers with a cigar
in his mouth." It can seem as though Winston

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.930
Churchill emerged on the world staged during
World War two more of less conjured by central

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:59.810
casting and full costume … The hat, the
bow tie, the overcoat, the cigar, the V sign.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:06.810
Very theatrical. In fact, Winston Churchill
spent a lifetime preparing for this role.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:15.699
The character and the creation were all his,
the consummation of a 65-year evolution.

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:20.120
As a proprietor of 'Chartwell Booksellers',
my tiny Churchill-centric bookshop in New

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:27.120
York City, for 32 years now been immersed
in all things Churchill. I've handled and

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:32.659
at least fleeting read everything that he
wrote and just about everything that was written

00:05:32.659 --> 00:05:36.370
about him. All that time, it never occurred
to me that I really might have anything to

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:40.830
add to the voluminous amount that has been
written about Winston Churchill, until one

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:51.800
day, it occurred to me that I did, 'Churchill
Style: The Art of Being Winston Churchill'

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:56.939
which fascinated me. The more I learned about
him, and I do still learn something new about

00:05:56.939 --> 00:06:03.789
him just about every day, the more fascinated
I became with the singular manner in which

00:06:03.789 --> 00:06:10.120
he lived his life away from politics. His
lifestyle I believe was cunningly constructed.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:18.600
It was no accident to nurture him and to sustain
him in a way that I can only describe as holistic.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:25.610
"Yes, Mr. Churchill was easily satisfied with
the best", as his friend, Effie Smith once

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:33.599
said about him. This was a quote that came
to be attached to Winston Churchill, but it

00:06:33.599 --> 00:06:38.710
was the first Lord Birkenhead, his close friend,
Effie Smith who said it about him. This does

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:46.979
not in any way dismiss the quotation. It was
true, but the elements of Churchill Style

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:56.579
seem to me to constitute a great deal more
than merely a lavish leisure life. One quote

00:06:56.590 --> 00:07:01.469
from all the endlessly quotable things that
Winston Churchill did in fact say stands out

00:07:01.469 --> 00:07:11.229
for me, "As one's fortunes are reduced, one's
spirit must expand to fill the void."

00:07:11.229 --> 00:07:18.229
He wrote these words in a letter to his wife,
Clementine in 1915 from the trenches in France,

00:07:19.099 --> 00:07:24.419
where he'd volunteer to serve after being
ousted his first Lord of the Admiralty over

00:07:24.419 --> 00:07:30.629
the disaster at Gallipoli for which I believe
he was unfairly scapegoated. Imagine, as "One's

00:07:30.629 --> 00:07:35.930
fortunes are reduced, one's spirit must expand
to fill the void form the trenches on the

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:44.890
Western front." I think it is a magnificent
maxim to live one's life by, and Churchill

00:07:44.969 --> 00:07:52.649
did. Now, by my count, he lost at least five
elections in his terribly bumpy political

00:07:52.659 --> 00:07:59.659
career. Each time that he was driven from
office, Churchill found something new in his

00:07:59.819 --> 00:08:06.819
private life to embrace that nurtured him,
and I believe rejuvenated him for the next

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:12.699
public battle.
After his resignation as first Lord, in the

00:08:12.699 --> 00:08:19.699
teeth of World War one, he discovered painting
as a pastime. This is one of his palettes

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:29.050
also buried in the attic at Chartwell. For
the rest of his life, he painted as a respite

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:35.880
from the world of politics. I just want to
point out here also, when I first saw this

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:42.090
picture of him, this was a cartoon that ran
and punch in the 1920s. I thought that was

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:45.670
a bottle of wine or a whisky that was next
to him, which would have been appropriate.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:48.990
Although, I don't think Churchill would have
actually painted with a bottle next to him,

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:57.100
but if you look closely, it says, 'Turpentine'.
After the tragic death of Winston Churchill's

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:04.100
two-year old daughter from illness in 1921,
he bought Chartwell. This is how it looked

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:11.320
when he first saw it, a rather ramshackle,
old country house in Kent, that Clementine

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.320
Churchill was desperately convinced would
ruin them. Churchill redesigned it, and slowly

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:25.620
rebuilt it very much with his own hands, turning
it into an oasis that he retreated to for

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:33.330
the rest of his life. He cultivated roses
and butterflies at Chartwell, with the intensity

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:40.190
of a full-time naturalist. He became so adept
at bricklaying that he actually joined the

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:47.190
bricklayers guild. They were not actually
that happy to have him.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:54.690
He was a staunch, anti-labor right, and I
don't believe he ever attended a meeting,

00:09:54.690 --> 00:10:01.660
but he did get his guild card. I've seen it.
Churchill played polo competitively until

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:08.660
he was 52 years old. He rode horses into his
80s. Bear in mind, as a young subaltern, he

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:18.580
was a cavalry officer. He built a horse racing
stable that enjoyed great success on the track

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:23.240
in the 1950s, much of it after his retirement
as Prime Minister. I adore that picture. I

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:27.620
think it's just beautiful. That's taken at
Chartwell.

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:38.340
He also smoked and drank. Did you know? He
did. He was a cigar epicure, and a champagne

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:45.440
aficionado whose greatest passions were Cuban
cigars and Pol Roger's champagne. He kept

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:52.030
his cigars in a small room on the second floor
at Chartwell between his bedroom and his study,

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:59.030
in boxes marked "You can't make this stuff
up", wrapped, naked and large for identification

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:07.730
purposes. Though, he received cigar cutters
as gifts, he did not use them. Churchill prefer

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:14.600
to gash his cigars at the tip in a small V-shape
appropriately, or he would pierce the cigar

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:19.220
with a large match, and then blow through
to create a passage.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:26.220
Pol Roger was Winston Churchill's parents'
favorite champagne. He inherited the preference,

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:36.110
and then like so much else in his life, he
bettered them. He bought staggering quantities

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:43.010
of Pol Roger from the family wine merchant,
Randolph Payne &amp; Sons at 61 St. James's Street.

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:50.010
This is just one and an early one at that
of a massive number of invoices that have

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:55.870
been preserved at the Churchill archives at
Churchill College Cambridge. It was a delight

00:11:55.870 --> 00:12:02.870
to run through them, but also rather frightening.
He would buy a Pol vintages.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:08.630
After a [virtue 00:12:03] of a lifetime of
drinking Pol Roger, Churchill finally met

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:14.360
Madame Odette Pol-Roger, the doyenne of the
champagne-making family at a British embassy,

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:21.360
armistice party in Paris in 1944. He would
later say that he was as captivated by Madame

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:26.930
Odette Pol-Roger's elegance and beauty as
he was by the champagne served at lunch that

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:33.510
day, Pol Roger 1928 which was his favorite
vintage. Churchill instructed the British

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:40.390
Ambassador, Alfred Duff Cooper to invite Madame
Pol-Roger to the embassy every time Churchill

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:46.610
was in Paris. Over the ensuing years, he would
dine with her at Buckingham Palace, he would

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:51.980
host her at Chartwell and at number 10 Downing
Street, and he visited her at the Pol Roger

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:58.980
Chateau at Epernay which Churchill called
'The world's most drinkable address'. He even

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:05.970
named one of his race horses after her, and
one of her champagne vintages after him.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:11.380
An interesting point to make about Winston
Churchill is that he very much enjoyed the

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:16.680
company of beautiful and intelligent women.
He love to bring them to his table. He kept

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:23.680
many, many close correspondences even with
old flames who had rejected him when he'd

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:31.260
propose marriage to them as a youth, but maintain
these very, very close friendships. Yet, as

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:38.460
far as I can tell and any other researcher
or a biographer can tell, Winston Churchill

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:44.030
never strayed from his marriage, probably
unique in the annals of British Politics.

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:49.630
He appears to have had no mistresses, but
he did have close friends. He enjoyed the

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:56.630
company of women who had something to say.
His favorite whisky was Johnnie Walker Black

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:04.060
Label or red. Hine was his favorite cognac.
It's interesting to note that although he

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:08.550
was a champagne aficionado, according to Christopher
Soames, his son-in-law, he didn't particularly

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:15.550
care about the caliber of cognac that he drank,
and at Chartwell, Soames often decanted into

00:14:16.250 --> 00:14:21.140
this. I couldn't believe, but I found this
decanter at Chartwell, the excellent vintages

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:25.720
of cognac that Churchill was presented with
by admirers, and replaced them with lesser

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:32.720
quality cognac which Churchill drank and enjoyed.
Now, I must confess to you, the original subtitle

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:41.160
for my book, 'Churchill Style', was 'Living
Well Beyond Your Means'. It truly was and

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:48.430
I was dissuaded from this by Sir Martin Gilbert
who said, "You can't."

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:56.270
However, Winston Churchill had a very hard
time holding on to money. His singular gift

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:03.480
I believe was a stalwart ability to live as
he wished, even if it was often beyond his

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:10.480
means. This was not solely a gift for extravagance,
nor did it ever quite ruin him. Churchill

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:19.480
simply knew how he wanted to live, and throughout
his long life, was endlessly resourceful in

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:26.750
finding ways to do so. He dressed above all
else for comfort, yet, he wore bespoke everything.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:36.670
He preferred to slip in and out of his clothes,
his dress shoes were made with zippers, not

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:43.670
laces, custom made, and after retiring his
Prime Minister in 1955, he practically lived

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:50.100
in his monogrammed slippers, these monogrammed
slippers which were of course hand-made.

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:57.100
The supreme example of his zipper-driven fashion
sense however was the siren suit that he himself

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:06.660
designed, a zip-up, all in one that his children
refer to as his 'Rompers'. Though ideally

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:15.230
suited to hurried dressing at the sound of
an air raid siren, the siren suit was in fact

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:22.230
conceived during Churchill's wilderness years
in the 1930s in imitation of the boilersuits

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:28.900
that the bricklayers wore, who he worked with
and admired so much. He however had a number

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:35.590
of these made up for him by the bespoke tailors
Turnbull &amp; Asser in different fabrics for

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:42.590
different activities. One in blue, another
green, and this burgundy velvet for formal

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:50.970
occasions. He wore this with metals.
He had a business like Blue Surge, and of

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:59.150
course, one constructed of [smack 00:16:55]
material for painting. Generously cut with

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:04.650
breast pockets and roomier side pockets pleats
to the trousers and fold over cuffs, these

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:11.650
siren suits were in apotheoses of a simple
practicality and fashion and comfort. We see

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:28.310
Churchill here demonstrating the siren suit
to General Eisenhower who looks fascinated.

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:35.310
As who wouldn't be?
The cook is going. Winston Churchill wrote

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:41.950
to Clementine in March, 1935. She sent in
her spoon and ladle on her own account, "I

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:47.790
am very glad. She had the knack to the highest
degree of making all food taste the same,

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:54.570
and not particularly good. I subsist on soup.”
These come from the kitchens at Chartwell.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:59.420
I think they're very handsome.
The Churchills eventually found a wonderful

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:05.980
new cook, a woman by the name of Georgina
Landemare, who was the wife of Paul Landemare,

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:11.910
the head chef at the Ritz. When Churchill
returned as first Lord of the Admiralty in

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:18.060
1935, Mrs. Landemare offered her services
to the Churchill family. This was an extraordinary

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:25.060
gesture on many counts, but particularly because
she was the most in demand catering chef in

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:32.310
London for all the finest dinner parties,
country house lunches. She was a very, very

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:38.620
important chef to the social scene, and she
was giving up an enormous amount of money

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:42.650
to come work for the Churchills.
She took up residency first the Admiralty

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:47.820
House, and then followed them to number ten
when Churchill became Prime Minister, working

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:54.820
miracles in a tiny basement kitchen at number
ten that was a dangerous war zone. The building

00:18:54.980 --> 00:19:01.540
was twice hit during the blitz, direct hits
that almost brought the kitchen down, and

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:07.110
there were fatalities in that kitchen. She
worked there throughout the war, never sleeping

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:12.680
until the Prime Minister did, which was a
difficult task, and always rising before he

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:18.990
did which was not that difficult. She would
cook for the Churchills until her own retirement

00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:25.990
in 1954 when she wrote this delightful cookbook.
Now, for many years, I've been saying truthfully

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:30.710
that I've only seen this cookbook a couple
of times. It's very rare, very hard to find,

00:19:30.710 --> 00:19:35.520
and very charming, and if you see it, you
should gran it. I have recently learned that

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:39.630
the Imperial War Museum, I'm delighted to
say is reissuing it in May in a paperback.

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:44.530
You will be able to purchase it either in
London or at Chartwell Booksellers, and I

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:50.050
recommend it.
Churchill's preferences in food. People often

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:56.260
ask me this question. They were fairly constant
as you might imagine. “In soup, only clear

00:19:56.260 --> 00:20:03.260
broths. It must be limpid", he once said.
In seafood, oysters and caviar, lobster and

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:10.180
dressed crab and scampi, dover sole and trout.
He did eat fish quite a bit, but meats, he

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:16.559
ate quite a bit of roast beef, shoulder of
lamb and Foie gras his favorite pudding, Yorkshire

00:20:16.559 --> 00:20:23.559
his favorite cheese, gruyere, and his favorite
dessert, yes, chocolate éclairs.

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:31.710
Even more than eating though, Churchill love
to talk around the dinner or lunch in table.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:38.970
Conversation at table I believe was his greatest
sport and pastime, a means of simultaneously

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:45.530
relaxing and sharpening his thinking, of letting
down his guard and taking in new information

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:52.530
from his guests. This delightful photograph
was taken at Chartwell in 1928 literally by

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:56.880
someone, a secretary who jumped up in front
of the table and snapped it in a moment. We

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:03.880
see Churchill staring straight at us. To his
right is the painter, Walter Sickert who was

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:10.250
a mentor and a tutor to Churchill. Churchill
had a number of great painter mentors who

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:16.100
helped him with his painting.
To Sickert's right is Clementine Churchill

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:23.100
in the hat, Diana Churchill their eldest daughter
beside her, a very young and quite adorable,

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:30.370
Randolph Churchill center, and Prof. Lindemann.
Frederick Lindemann sits to Randolph's right,

00:21:32.260 --> 00:21:39.260
the gentleman with the mustache. He was Churchill's
... One of his closest confidants, and his

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:46.370
man for all things scientific and technological.
Lindemann practically lived at Chartwell,

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:51.170
and even was given a post, an official poster
in World War two, and helped Churchill device

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:58.170
a number of the very clever and often intricate
technological innovations that I wouldn't

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:04.650
say that he designed them, but he certainly
would push for them and inspire them in his

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:11.650
staff, including the floating bridges that
were used, the floating landing pads that

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:20.710
were used at D-Day invasion. Prof. Lindemann
was a very important part of that.

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:26.600
Beside Prof, sort of glaring askance, looking
slightly askance is Eddie Marsh who was Churchill's

00:22:26.600 --> 00:22:33.050
longest lived in terms of service, private
secretary and a very important man in his

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:40.050
life for many years. Beside Eddie Marsh is
Diana [Mitford 00:22:39] who was another one

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:45.280
of those beautiful and intelligent women that
Churchill just enjoyed having around at table,

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:52.070
and next to her is Thérèse Sickert, Walter
Sickert's wife. Churchill believed in face-to-face

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:59.070
conversation more than anything else, and
the power of his own personal charm. During

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:06.000
World War two, his appetite for conversation
and social contact with Stalin and Roosevelt

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:11.530
helped build the alliances that shaped the
outcome of the war really as much as anything

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:16.180
that occurred on the battlefields. I love
this picture for many reasons. One of them

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:19.850
is that the ring that Churchill is wearing
if you look closely is actually the cigar

00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:26.850
band from the cigar that he is smoking.
Churchill's willingness to travel anywhere

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:37.870
to make this social contact throughout the
war, despite his health and his advancing

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:44.870
years yielded three titanic meetings of ‘The
Big Three’ as we know, Tehran in 1943 which

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:52.760
is pictured here, Yalta in 1945, and the final
face-to-face after Roosevelt's death with

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:59.500
Harry Truman and Stalin at Potsdam in 1945
as well. Churchill was often accompanied on

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:05.840
these travels by his wife, Clementine or even
more often by one of his daughters, Mary or

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:12.840
here, we see Sarah Churchill being introduced
to Franklin Roosevelt who's clearly charmed.

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:20.330
Churchill preferred to fly, and was a pilot
himself and one of the creators really of

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:28.080
the REF. He often took the controls for a
short period usually while the plane was still

00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:37.170
on the ground. Whether flying or sailing the
Atlantic, the perils of these journeys were

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:46.030
enormous with his age and health adding greatly
to those dangers. None of the planes that

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:52.040
Churchill flew in during World War two were
pressurized.

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:58.240
Susceptible to pneumonia, he kept close by
him a specially constructed oxygen mask that

00:24:58.240 --> 00:25:05.240
allowed him to take oxygen and smoke a cigar
at the same time. In Cairo, as Churchill was

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:13.950
about to board a small plane for a flights
to inspect the troops at El Alamein, he asked

00:25:13.950 --> 00:25:19.730
aloud if he could spoke onboard. A young RAF
officer very nervously, and after much hesitation

00:25:19.730 --> 00:25:25.380
volunteered to check whether the auxiliary
gas tanks were full. Churchill stopped him.

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:32.380
"I enjoy a cigar", he told the young man,
"But not at the risk of being incinerated."

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:39.050
In August, 1929, Winston Churchill embarked
on a three-month tour of the United States

00:25:39.050 --> 00:25:46.050
and Canada by train to see the country as
he told his good friend, American financier,

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:54.720
Bernard Baruch to see the country and meet
the leaders of its fortunes. He was accompanied

00:25:54.720 --> 00:26:01.720
by his son, Randolph in the riding boots,
then on the left his brother, Jack on right,

00:26:03.130 --> 00:26:10.130
and Jack's son, Johnny. I'm afraid and I looked
this up with hope that he never made it to

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:19.350
Denver, though, he did come close. On September
18, 1929, Churchill received an invitation

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:28.500
to dine with the Mile High Club here in Denver.
Churchill cabled back a couple of days later

00:26:29.290 --> 00:26:34.500
that he could not make it.
Now, between you and me, Churchill was at

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:41.500
that moment in Hollywood, being wined and
dined by William Randolph Hearst, Marion Davies

00:26:42.460 --> 00:26:47.250
and Louis B. Mayer. This picture was taken
on a movie set that's not New York City. That's

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:53.480
just a set Churchill was given a tour. Churchill
adored movies. He watched them throughout

00:26:53.480 --> 00:27:00.280
the war to relax at checkers.
He wrote screenplays in the 1930s for the

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:07.280
[Carter 00:27:00] brothers to make ends meet.
His most promising screenplay, none of them

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:11.500
were ever filmed. In some ways, the [Carter
00:27:09] Brothers were just hiring him to

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:17.880
help him out, but these screenplays survived,
and he did write one, and as you must know

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:24.799
about Napoleon. He tried to convince ... While
he was in Hollywood at a dinner party, he

00:27:24.799 --> 00:27:28.720
spent almost the entire night trying to convince
Charlie Chaplin to play Napoleon, which seems

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:34.640
to me an inspired idea, and in some ways,
you have to think that Chaplin took this to

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:40.960
heart later on.
I'm up for ... Although I said that already,

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:47.960
Churchill took a number of transatlantic voyages
when he wasn't flying throughout his life,

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:56.150
most of them on Cunard-White Star Line ships.
In November, 1895, weeks before his 21st birthday,

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:05.950
he sailed to New York for the very first time
on the Cunard Royal Mail Steamship Etruria.

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:11.770
He did not have a very good time. He didn't
enjoy sailing at all which is ironic when

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:15.360
you consider he was first Lord of the Admiralty
twice.

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:21.880
"I do not contemplate ever taking a sea voyage
for pleasure", he wrote to his mother from

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:27.270
the ship. "I shall always look upon journeys
by sea as necessary evils which have to be

00:28:27.270 --> 00:28:34.270
undergone to carry out some definite plan."
Usually, Churchill sailed on either the Queen

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:42.440
Elizabeth or the Queen Mary. In March, 1946,
it was the Queen Elizabeth that he took here

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:49.010
to the United States to deliver what would
come to be known as his 'Iron Curtain Speech'

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:54.190
in Fulton, Missouri.
During the course of that voyage, he agreed

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:59.710
that Cunard's request to publish, to write
a foreword for a war history they hope to

00:28:59.710 --> 00:29:04.370
publish of the two queens. They were troop
carriers in the war, the largest troop carriers

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:11.370
ever seen, which was very dangerous as you
can imagine. They were a big target. "Built

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:19.460
for the arts of peace and to link the old
world with the new, the queens challenged

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:23.890
the fury of Hitlerism in the Battle of the
Atlantic,” Churchill’s foreword began.

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:29.540
“At a speed never before realized in war,
they carried over a million to defend the

00:29:29.540 --> 00:29:34.840
liberties of civilization, to the men who
contributed to the success of our operations

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:40.290
in the years of peril, and to those who brought
these two great ships into existence. The

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:44.520
world owes a debt that it will not be easy
to ..."

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:51.520
As you see, Churchill paused here to change
one word in his final draft, the last word

00:29:53.480 --> 00:30:02.440
'Repay'. Instead of “The world owes a debt
that it will not be easy to repay,” Churchill

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:08.640
rewrote his conclusion to read, “The world
owes a debt that it will not be easy to measure.”

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:17.120
I like that word ‘Measure'. In terms of
Churchill's style, it's what I was after and

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:22.880
what I've tried to accomplish, to measure
Winston Churchill not in terms of the debt

00:30:22.880 --> 00:30:29.870
we all owe him as a historical figure, but
rather, to measure the man as a man, through

00:30:29.870 --> 00:30:36.870
the extravagantly artful style with which
he lived his life, the Churchill style. Thank

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:52.260
you. (Applause)
Female: Thank you very much. Thank you very

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:58.340
much, Mr. Singer. We now have time for a few
questions. Please raise your hand, and one

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:08.260
of us will bring a mic to you.
Barry: No questions? We covered it. Richard.

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:11.450
Oh God. That's scary.
Male: Barry, you said in rummaging in the

00:31:11.450 --> 00:31:18.450
attic of Chartwell, when did you do that and
how did you get in?

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:27.309
Barry: They didn't know I was there. No. I
went and asked them if I could look through

00:31:27.309 --> 00:31:33.419
the inventories that they had of stuff that
wasn't on display, and they didn't even ... No

00:31:33.419 --> 00:31:38.380
one really had looked at them very often before
then. I just marked up these lists, and then

00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:44.130
they took me up or down, and we just looked
for the stuff. They have a lot of amazing

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:49.970
things that they became aware of themselves
I think just through our rummaging around,

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:52.370
and some of that stuff I think is now come
out.

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:59.370
I mean, when they closed up the house, Winston
Churchill owned Chartwell his entire life,

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:06.960
but ultimately through an arrangement that
was worked out with the National Trust, where

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:12.610
they basically bought the house from him in
the 1950s after he left off as his Prime Minister,

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:15.610
but allowed him to live there and Clementine
to live there until the end of their lives,

00:32:15.610 --> 00:32:22.559
and when they were finished with Chartwell,
it was immediately turned into a museum. Everything

00:32:22.559 --> 00:32:27.890
is still there. I don't know if you've been
there. I hardly recommend that you make it

00:32:27.890 --> 00:32:31.290
a journey to Chartwell.
I can't imagine that there is another home

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:38.290
in the world that has the spirit so palpably
present of its inhabit or Winston Churchill.

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:44.760
It's really an amazing place. Rather unpretentious.
Not a grand country house, and there's a lot

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:51.760
of stuff in the attic.
Male: Can you talk a little bit about his

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:59.570
standing desk and his writing style in terms
of Churchill, and also his love of military

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:08.390
uniforms that I've read that he designed himself
over and above the siren suits?

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:15.540
Barry: He did love uniforms. It's true. In
some ways, I think he became a soldier just

00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:21.490
to be able to wear one. He enjoyed wearing
them. He was proud of them. His designing

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:25.880
of the uniforms, I think he had a hand in
it, but I don't think it was a primary design

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:29.370
concern.
The standing desk is a fascinating thing.

00:33:29.370 --> 00:33:35.429
The standing desk that is at Chartwell now
was a gift that the children and grandchildren

00:33:35.429 --> 00:33:40.160
presented to Churchill to replace the one
that he really spent most of his time working

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:47.160
at. Churchill did work standing up, pacing
and dictating. It all came from here, and

00:33:47.380 --> 00:33:53.220
he was improvising as he wandered. What was
really was the standing desk was a rather

00:33:53.220 --> 00:34:00.220
short desk, just sat, laid up that a local
wood maker had built for Churchill who lived

00:34:01.090 --> 00:34:05.549
near Chartwell. It stood on the table.
Again, very unglamorous. There's now a very

00:34:05.549 --> 00:34:10.200
beautiful carved one at Chartwell, which Churchill
did work at, but most of his writing was at

00:34:10.200 --> 00:34:17.200
the standing desk. He had the secretaries
who were with him. Throughout the nighttime,

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:23.009
he dined late, he prepared himself to work,
and then he went to work late, and he just

00:34:23.009 --> 00:34:28.089
stood at that desk and worked. He corrected
proofs at the desk. He did very little.

00:34:28.089 --> 00:34:32.289
Although, you see him here actually in this
photograph. It's not up right now, but the

00:34:32.289 --> 00:34:36.190
original photograph that I had up, you just
see him sitting at a desk at Chartwell, and

00:34:36.190 --> 00:34:40.339
he is looking at proofs, and obviously, he
sat down on occasion, but he did a lot of

00:34:40.339 --> 00:34:47.339
work, most of his work standing up. I have
to [defer 00:34:46] to them.

00:34:53.389 --> 00:34:56.299
Female: You said that he did not come to Denver.
Did he visit anywhere in Colorado?

00:34:56.299 --> 00:35:01.589
Barry: No. Not that I can tell. He blew right
past the state unfortunately.

00:35:01.589 --> 00:35:08.589
Female: What would you say inspired you to
go study Churchill?

00:35:10.989 --> 00:35:17.989
Barry: For 32 years, I've been selling Churchill's
books. I was always intrigued by the man,

00:35:20.599 --> 00:35:25.380
but I've learned most of what I know by working
with those books over the years. I was not

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:31.390
a Churchill scholar when I began. I was simply
an admirer. When I opened Chartwell, we named

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:36.140
the store 'Chartwell' without necessarily
having an intention initially to be a Churchill

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:40.630
bookshop. It's not like that was the flash
idea. It just became clear to me ...

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:47.529
We went to a lovely, small, select English
library style bookshop in Midtown Manhattan,

00:35:47.529 --> 00:35:51.319
sort of an oasis, but in the first year, it
became clear to me that with the name, we

00:35:51.319 --> 00:35:56.979
should have some Churchill books. I brought
a few in. I took a very tiny ad out at the

00:35:56.979 --> 00:36:01.859
back of the New Yorker saying, "Write for
our Winston Churchill catalog". Which I didn't

00:36:01.859 --> 00:36:08.859
have, but I was curious to see if anybody
would write back, and they did. It became

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:15.369
clear that it was something that I should
pursue which I enjoyed very much pursuing.

00:36:15.369 --> 00:36:20.599
It couldn't have been the more interesting
or more prolific public figure.

00:36:20.599 --> 00:36:25.059
Then, when I went to London for the first
time to start buying for the store, it became

00:36:25.059 --> 00:36:30.130
clear to me that while every store bookshop
in London seem to have a shelf of Winston

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:36.349
Churchill, mo one was specializing in Churchill.
I also have a theory ... People ask me, "Still,

00:36:36.349 --> 00:36:40.739
shouldn't there be a Winston Churchill bookshop
in London? Why should it be in New York City?"

00:36:40.739 --> 00:36:46.440
In some ways, Churchill was far more popular
in the United States than he is in London.

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:52.869
We view him from the prism solely of having
saved us and the world in western civilization

00:36:52.869 --> 00:36:57.450
World War two which is a marvelous prism to
see him through and Churchill at his best,

00:36:57.450 --> 00:37:04.450
but that 65-year career had a lot of ... He
offended a lot of people along the way. He

00:37:04.849 --> 00:37:10.470
stepped on a lot of feet. He changed parties.
I think that there is a consistency which

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:14.079
I will not get into here now, but there is
a consistency to Churchill's political positions

00:37:14.079 --> 00:37:21.079
throughout his life, but the English see him
in a much longer view, and from a much more

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:27.479
weary and slightly dubious view.
Chartwell Booksellers actually belongs in

00:37:27.479 --> 00:37:32.109
New York. Also, Churchill was half American.
Very proud of the fact. His mother was born

00:37:32.109 --> 00:37:39.109
in Brooklyn. His grandfather, Leonard Jerome
owned 'The New York Times' at one point, and

00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:47.289
he was very proud of the fact that he wasn't
just a descendant of the Duke of Marlborough

00:37:47.289 --> 00:37:53.579
and a [tough 00:37:49]. He was also an American
which he thought gave him a real sense of

00:37:53.579 --> 00:38:00.269
independence which he did have from his class
from class demands, and it's another reason

00:38:00.269 --> 00:38:07.269
why a lot of his fellow conservative politicians
didn't trust him.

00:38:11.420 --> 00:38:18.420
Male: I too can identify with Churchill's
habit of working at a stand up desk, and I've

00:38:19.529 --> 00:38:24.509
done that all my life. My question is, did
Churchill's paintings have any commercial

00:38:24.509 --> 00:38:29.739
value, and do they have commercial value today?
Barry: They have no commercial value at all

00:38:29.739 --> 00:38:36.140
today. If you have any, please bring them
to me, and I'll be glad to take them off your

00:38:36.140 --> 00:38:43.140
hands. Churchill I don't believe ever sold
a painting. He gave them to his family, and

00:38:44.119 --> 00:38:51.119
he gave them to friends on occasion as gifts.
He painted over 500. Close to 550, right?

00:38:53.559 --> 00:38:59.479
Most of those still are hanging in the painting
studio at Chartwell, though they are claimed

00:38:59.479 --> 00:39:03.829
in some sense. Groups of them belong to different
children and now, grandchildren, and then

00:39:03.829 --> 00:39:09.910
great grandchildren. They all remain in the
family. They are very good. Certainly not

00:39:09.910 --> 00:39:16.170
all of the same quality, but there is a charm
and a vivacity to them, and sometimes, they're

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:19.989
actually brilliant paintings.
He was very good. Very prolific. He painted

00:39:19.989 --> 00:39:25.239
to relax. He loved bright colors more than
anything else. They're very appealing, and

00:39:25.239 --> 00:39:31.839
lately, they have begun to come on the market.
Some of the finest paintings probably that

00:39:31.839 --> 00:39:37.700
I think we will ever see from that group of
Churchill that painted, Lady Mary Soames when

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:44.700
she past, the family auctioned them in December.
I went to London and went to Sotheby's before

00:39:44.729 --> 00:39:49.029
they auctioned just to see them. I went down
in the basement, and they were just lined

00:39:49.029 --> 00:39:53.569
up on the wall, and I just wanted to see them
before they disappeared ... Who knew where

00:39:53.569 --> 00:39:58.400
they were going, who's going to buy them and
where they were going to go? Most of them

00:39:58.400 --> 00:40:04.109
sold for around a million, and one of them
sold for two million, so they have become

00:40:04.109 --> 00:40:15.149
extremely valuable and rare.
Male: Mr. Singer. Would you be kind enough

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:25.560
to tell us a little bit about each of Winston's
children and how they were perceived by Winston

00:40:25.660 --> 00:40:32.180
and Clementine, and how they fit into his
life?

00:40:32.180 --> 00:40:42.089
Barry: That's another book. In brief, Winston
Churchill doted on his children. Some would

00:40:42.089 --> 00:40:52.649
say to an over ... He overindulged them, particularly
Randolph who he adored and took with him from

00:40:52.690 --> 00:40:58.450
very young age. Churchill was also traveling
a lot without his children, so they didn't

00:40:58.450 --> 00:41:02.579
see him that much. It was a very complicated
thing when he would come back home and spend

00:41:02.579 --> 00:41:09.579
time with them. There were brief bursts.
For better of worsts, with the exception of

00:41:10.229 --> 00:41:19.669
the youngest, Mary, they all struggled as
adults to varying degrees. Randolph is a case

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:29.140
study in the travails of the only son of a
great man. Randolph had a lot of gifts. Randolph

00:41:29.200 --> 00:41:33.229
was as you can see from some of these pictures,
an extraordinarily handsome, young man. He

00:41:33.229 --> 00:41:38.049
wanted to go into politics, but Randolph thought
he could do everything immediately. His father

00:41:38.049 --> 00:41:44.079
had raised him in a way that he just thought,
"I can follow and better him instantly."

00:41:44.079 --> 00:41:47.900
I would imagine that to some degree, Winston
Churchill himself was that sort of a young

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:55.779
man when he was coming up. This is a very
odd statement to make, but Churchill spoke

00:41:55.779 --> 00:42:02.059
to it himself. In some ways, he was blessed
by the fact that his father died young. His

00:42:02.059 --> 00:42:09.059
father was an enormous political figure, and
a very, very important and admired, and rather

00:42:09.259 --> 00:42:16.259
loud political figure in London. Because he
died young, the world opened up to Winston

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:21.920
Churchill for him to become an individual
at a very young age, and to find himself,

00:42:21.920 --> 00:42:26.059
and to make something of it himself because
he also was left very little money, so Churchill

00:42:26.059 --> 00:42:33.059
didn't suffer in the way his children did
by a surfeit of too much attention.

00:42:34.489 --> 00:42:40.650
They struggled. They all had with the exception
of Mary problems with alcohol. Winston Churchull

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:47.650
consumed a great deal of alcohol without having
it affect him very much if at all. His children

00:42:48.460 --> 00:42:55.460
had affected problematically. Randolph, Diana
and Sarah were all alcoholics.

00:42:55.749 --> 00:43:02.200
They've had a very prominent place in his
life. I think the best moment for all of them

00:43:02.200 --> 00:43:11.479
was World War two individually. Randolph volunteered
for the most dangerous espionage missions

00:43:11.479 --> 00:43:18.479
to prove himself, and did prove himself. Both
Sarah and Mary accompanied Churchill again

00:43:18.739 --> 00:43:23.710
and again on these very dangerous and important
journeys, and I think were very close to him

00:43:23.710 --> 00:43:30.630
and grew very close to him in that. Diana
was a very sad situation. She ultimately committed

00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:37.349
suicide.
When she was in her 50s and Churchill was

00:43:37.349 --> 00:43:44.349
past 80, it was devastating for the family.
Sarah became a very successful actress for

00:43:44.749 --> 00:43:48.059
a while. I don't know if you've ever seen
'The Royal Family', but she danced with Fred

00:43:48.059 --> 00:43:59.789
Astaire and held her own, but her later life
was also troubled by alcohol. It was tough.

00:43:59.789 --> 00:44:04.569
Most people seem to feel, and I believe it's
true that Clementine devoted herself to her

00:44:04.569 --> 00:44:10.349
husband in a singular way, because he was
a singular man and needed a lot of attention,

00:44:10.349 --> 00:44:15.609
but neglected the children as a result. They
had to compete with him for her attention.

00:44:15.609 --> 00:44:19.420
Complicated.
Male: Marigold?

00:44:19.420 --> 00:44:28.769
Barry: Marigold died at two and a half of
Septostomy, and both Churchills were away

00:44:28.769 --> 00:44:35.769
when she became ill. They had left her with
a French nanny, but ultimately, Clementine

00:44:36.460 --> 00:44:42.219
as any mother would blamed herself for leaving
her with a nurse who wasn't able to deal with

00:44:42.219 --> 00:44:48.160
the fact that she was ill. By the time they
got back, it was too late. A very mild child

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:54.329
with illness suddenly metastasized very quickly,
and she was gone. I can't even bring myself

00:44:54.329 --> 00:45:01.329
to think ... I have two daughters, what that
must have been like for them for the rest

00:45:04.130 --> 00:45:08.979
of their lives.
Female: Hi. Is there anybody in today's political

00:45:08.979 --> 00:45:15.979
landscape that jumps out at you as being Churchillesque?
Barry: No one even wonders out at me. However,

00:45:21.900 --> 00:45:28.900
I must say I was fortunate enough to spend
time with Boris Johnson, the Mayor of London

00:45:29.390 --> 00:45:34.460
recently. When his book came out, we had a
reception for him at Chartwell, and we had

00:45:34.460 --> 00:45:41.160
a very interesting evening where he spoke.
The man has a great deal of substance. I think

00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:45.219
he is truly an intellectual which is very
... I can't think of enough, virtually another

00:45:45.219 --> 00:45:50.640
politician who you can say that about today.
He knows a great deal about Winston Churchill

00:45:50.640 --> 00:45:56.239
which endears him to me, but he also fancies
himself as Winston Churchill, and whether

00:45:56.239 --> 00:46:03.239
that endears him to the British public remains
to be seen. He's an interesting fellow and

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:12.190
clownish in a way that I believe is very cleverly
conceived. He's the first person that leaps

00:46:15.079 --> 00:46:22.079
to mind when you ask that question.
Male: You've spoken extensively mentioning

00:46:24.249 --> 00:46:30.359
Chartwell and the events there. What role
and what events occurred at Blenheim Castle?

00:46:30.359 --> 00:46:35.759
Wasn't this another of his residences?
Barry: It was not one of his residences, and

00:46:35.759 --> 00:46:42.759
the only major event is that he was born there
by accident. His mother was pregnant but not

00:46:44.609 --> 00:46:51.609
due. She was there for a weekend of parties
and riding, and she went into labor. There's

00:46:54.579 --> 00:46:58.999
a lot to that story. You could write a book
about Jenny Churchill going into labor at

00:46:58.999 --> 00:47:04.039
Blenheim Palace. Whether it was premature,
whether it was that Churchill actually was

00:47:04.039 --> 00:47:10.059
being born full term, and that he was conceived
out of wedlock, there's a lot of speculation

00:47:10.059 --> 00:47:17.059
about that. Fundamentally, in the midst of
a dance, she went into labor on I think on

00:47:18.019 --> 00:47:23.390
a Saturday night, and they had to lay her
down in a cloakroom, and she gave birth rather

00:47:23.390 --> 00:47:29.239
unexpectedly to Winston Churchill who burst
upon the world.

00:47:29.239 --> 00:47:34.479
He grew up there. He did spend a lot of time
there. His parents interestingly enough were

00:47:34.479 --> 00:47:40.119
seldom around when he was growing up. They
were very busy, socially, politically. He

00:47:40.119 --> 00:47:45.849
was left at Blenheim Palace a lot, a terrific
place to grow up. He proposed to his wife,

00:47:45.849 --> 00:47:52.049
Clementine at Blenheim Palace, but it was
not a residence. He didn't live there.

00:47:52.049 --> 00:47:59.029
His father was the non-inheriting son of the
Duke of Marlborough. I think the second living

00:47:59.029 --> 00:48:05.440
son, the third son, the son between passed
away, but he didn't ... Churchill by a long

00:48:05.440 --> 00:48:11.440
stretch was an heir to that title but never
came close.

00:48:11.440 --> 00:48:18.440
Male: From Churchill's art to his speeches,
his writings, where he lived, what would you

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:28.900
say was the theme of his life from your perspective,
and then from perhaps Churchill's own perspective?

00:48:34.109 --> 00:48:41.109
Barry: One of themes was the richness of the
history and the language of the English-speaking

00:48:42.700 --> 00:48:46.509
peoples. He wrote about that again and again,
and probably no one else ever wrote about

00:48:46.509 --> 00:48:53.509
it so eloquently. I also think that a central
tenet of Churchill's life, and it's woven

00:48:55.519 --> 00:49:02.519
into his writing is magnanimity. Churchill
had a great deal of magnanimity for his enemies.

00:49:08.499 --> 00:49:15.499
He forgave very quickly once a confrontation
was over, and he felt that it was a central

00:49:15.729 --> 00:49:22.729
tenet of statesmanship. He had no greater
enemy in the run-up to World War two, the

00:49:23.099 --> 00:49:30.099
Neville Chamberlain.
Yet, when Chamberlain collapsed when his efforts

00:49:30.390 --> 00:49:37.390
failed, and when Churchill was designated
as his successor, Churchill remained at Admiralty

00:49:38.900 --> 00:49:48.340
House for a number of weeks to allow Chamberlain
to graceful exit number 10 Downing Street,

00:49:48.349 --> 00:49:54.779
and then, he invited Chamberlain into his
cabinet. That was a very central tenet of

00:49:54.779 --> 00:50:01.779
Churchill's way of viewing the world, that
we're all in this together in a sense, that

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:08.920
mistakes are made. They must be remedied.
They must be addressed, and then they must

00:50:10.249 --> 00:50:22.380
be forgiven for us to live together.
On that note, let me just say that there were

00:50:22.380 --> 00:50:28.769
a lot of things thrown around here last night
very brilliantly on Mr. Krauthammer, but there

00:50:28.769 --> 00:50:35.769
were a lot of facts that were complicated,
more complicated and some erroneous, I only

00:50:37.019 --> 00:50:43.660
wanted to correct two regarding Winston Churchill.
You should know that his autobiography, his

00:50:43.660 --> 00:50:48.940
only volume of autobiography is my early life,
not my early years. It's important to remember

00:50:48.940 --> 00:50:55.940
so you can come to Chartwell and ask for it.
The other is that when Churchill ...

00:50:56.630 --> 00:51:01.479
This is an interesting thing just to know
that when Churchill was hit by a car in New

00:51:01.479 --> 00:51:08.479
York City, he was not hit by a taxi. He stepped
out of a taxi cab, while looking for the apartment

00:51:09.269 --> 00:51:14.170
of Bernard Baruch where he was going for an
after dinner drink, looked the wrong way,

00:51:14.170 --> 00:51:20.039
and was actually struck by a small truck which
is why he almost died, but it was not a taxi

00:51:20.039 --> 00:51:23.869
cab. Just those things were hanging around
me last night, and I wanted to share them

00:51:23.869 --> 00:51:26.440
with you, and thank you very much for having
me. (Applause)

